# hostname -> PRIVATE VPC address, for URL health checks.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES. When PRIVATE_ROUTE_ENABLED=true, a health check for any hostname
# listed here is sent to that private address over http instead of going out to the
# public internet. The request never leaves the VPC: it costs no egress, and it never
# reaches the site's AWS WAF — the WAF that forced VERIFY_MAX_REQUESTS_PER_SECOND to 5.
# A hostname that is NOT listed here is unaffected and keeps using public https.
#
# COPY THIS TO private-hosts.conf (same directory) ON THE BOX. The real file is
# gitignored on purpose: it is infrastructure topology, it changes when servers change,
# and it must never require an image rebuild. Both the backend and worker containers
# bind-mount this directory read-only at /etc/sitemap, and re-read the file within a
# minute of any change — no restart needed.
#
# FORMAT: /etc/hosts, verbatim. `IP hostname [hostname ...]`, `#` starts a comment,
# blank lines ignored. That is deliberate — the content below is what `pm2 list` on each
# box already produces, so it can be pasted with no reformatting step to get wrong.
#
# BOTH SPELLINGS ARE HANDLED. If only `www.example.com` is listed, a probe of
# `example.com` still finds it (and the reverse). Only the `www.` label, never an
# arbitrary subdomain — `shop.example.com` is a different site and is never assumed.
#
# ONE HOSTNAME, TWO IPs = NOT ROUTED. A hostname listed under two different addresses is
# dropped from the map entirely and keeps using public https, rather than silently
# picking the first. There is no way to tell from a response that a plausible 200 came
# from the wrong box, so an ambiguous entry is refused instead of guessed. Check
# `GET /api/private-routes` — its `conflicts` field lists every one, and the boot log
# names them with line numbers.
#
#   >>> THE DATA BELOW HAS ONE: www.industrialworld360.com appears under both
#   >>> 10.0.61.203 (sites01) and 10.0.49.183 (sites02). It will NOT be routed
#   >>> privately until one of the two lines is removed. Confirm with devops which
#   >>> server actually hosts it.
#
# BEFORE ENABLING, per IP: prove the origin selects a vhost from the Host header (two
# hostnames on one IP must give different responses), and prove a real deep URL returns
# the same status AND the same page privately as publicly. An IP that fails either check
# is simply left out of this file — no code change and no flag needed for a partial
# rollout. Commands are in docs/deployment/deploy-v1.63-Live.txt.

